====================================================================================================
(A) Duplication: the cn-MAP depends only on the empirical distribution; fixed priors tend to ML
====================================================================================================
valid trials (some program with finite fit): 1998 of 2000; c cycles through [0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.3]
cn-MAP set on the replicated sample h^(r), r = 2,3,7,50, equal to the c n0-MAP set on h: 1998 of 1998
samples with no c n0-MAP among the ML programs: 671
  of these, at r = floor(r_th)+1 the w-MAP set is disjoint from the cn-MAP set (the claim): 671
  (at that r the w-MAP set already lies inside the ML set in 594; not claimed, other programs may need larger r)
  largest r_th = 11398.1
samples where some c n0-MAP is an ML program (no contradiction from duplication): 1327

====================================================================================================
(B) Two programs: coin (length 1) against a deterministic f (length 1+k), data labelled by f
====================================================================================================
k = 10. J_cn(coin) = c n + n, J_cn(f) = c n (1+k); J_lam(coin) = lam + n, J_lam(f) = lam (1+k).
       c           cn-MAP at n=1,10,1e3,1e6   predicted: coin iff c k > 1
    1/20                         f, f, f, f   c k = 1/2
    1/10                 tie, tie, tie, tie   c k = 1
     1/5             coin, coin, coin, coin   c k = 2
  lambda     least n with f the unique lambda-MAP   predicted: n > lambda k
       1                                       11   lambda k = 10
       2                                       21   lambda k = 20
       5                                       51   lambda k = 50
So for c > 1/k the cn-MAP keeps the coin (0-1 error 1/2 on f-labelled data) at every n,
while every fixed lambda (prior 2^{-lambda l}) switches to f (error 0) after lambda k samples.

